.TH sa "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
sa \- process accounting
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsa [\-abcjlmnrstu] [\-v\fIN\fB] [\fIfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
sa -- process accounting
Usage:	sa [ options ] [ file ]
Options:
	-a	Commands seen once or unprintable called '***other'
	-b	Sort by average CPU time per call
	-c	Print CPU time as percentage of all CPU time used
	-j	Print average times per call, not totals
	-l	Separate user and system times
	-m	Information per user, not per command
	-n	Sort by number of calls
	-r	Reverse sort
	-s	Condense the information
	-t	Print CPU time as percentage of real time
	-u	Print user and command directly from raw file
	-vN	If called no more than 'N' times, put it into '**junk**'
The default 'file' is '/usr/adm/acct'.
.HE
.B sa
reads the raw accounting records written by the kernel's
.B acct()
mechanism from
.I file
(default
.BR /usr/adm/acct )
and prints, for each distinct command name, the number of times it was
called, its total CPU time (user plus system), and its total real time.
Output is sorted by decreasing CPU time unless
.B \-b
or
.B \-n
is given.
Before scanning the raw file,
.B sa
reads the summary files
.B /usr/adm/savacct
and
.B /usr/adm/usracct
so that totals accumulate across runs.
.PP
If
.B \-u
is given,
.B sa
instead prints, for every record in
.IR file ,
the user name and command name directly, and does no summarizing at
all.
.PP
Options:
.IP \fB\-a\fR
Commands that were called only once, or whose name contains a
non-printable character, are folded into the single category
.BR ***other .
.IP \fB\-b\fR
Sort by average CPU time per call, instead of by total CPU time.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Also print each command's CPU time as a percentage of the total CPU
time used by all commands.
.IP \fB\-j\fR
Print average time per call rather than totals for each command.
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Print user time and system time in separate columns, instead of their
sum.
.IP \fB\-m\fR
Accumulate and print statistics per user rather than per command.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Sort by number of calls, instead of by CPU time.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Reverse the sort order.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
After the raw file has been read and summarized, merge the results
back into
.B /usr/adm/savacct
and
.BR /usr/adm/usracct ,
and truncate
.IR file .
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Also print each command's CPU time as a percentage of its real time.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Print the user name and command name directly from the raw accounting
file, one line per record, rather than a summary.
.IP \fB\-v\fIN\fR
For any command called no more than
.I N
times, ask (on standard input) whether it should be moved into the
category
.BR **junk** .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/usr/adm/acct\fR	raw accounting records (default)
\fB/usr/adm/savacct\fR	saved per-command summary
\fB/usr/adm/usracct\fR	saved per-user summary
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B accton
.SH NOTES
.B /usr/adm/acct
grows without bound while accounting is enabled; run
.B "sa \-s"
periodically to condense it.
